lesson.fix:
The fix is to implement a strict allowlist for all user-facing update endpoints. Instead of mapping the entire incoming JSON object to the database, the code should explicitly extract only the fields the user is permitted to change. For example, in a Node.js/Express app, instead of 'User.update(req.body)', use 'User.update({ firstName: req.body.firstName, lastName: req.body.lastName })'. Sensitive fields like 'c_isCustomerVerified' should only be modified by internal logic triggered by a valid verification token link.

```javascript
// FIXED CODE EXAMPLE
app.patch('/api/profile', (req, res) => {
  const allowedUpdates = ['firstName', 'lastName', 'phoneNumber'];
  const updates = {};

  // Only copy fields that are in the allowlist
  allowedUpdates.forEach(field => {
    if (req.body[field] !== undefined) {
      updates[field] = req.body[field];
    }
  });

  // This prevents 'c_isCustomerVerified' from being updated by the user
  db.users.update({ id: req.user.id }, { $set: updates });
  res.status(200).send('Profile updated');
});
```
